### Part II. Core Excerpts on Hierarchy, Social Distance, and Communication

#### Excerpt 1: Hierarchical Relations and Social Distance (Chapter 7, pp. 159–160)

> **HIERARCHICAL AND PEER RELATIONS**
> 
> **Testing a Hypothesis.** Much of the work done in the field of formal organizations emphasizes two different facets of these social systems: the impersonality and emotional detachment of relations between individuals and groups on different hierarchical levels, and the cohesiveness and informality of relations among peers on the same organizational level. This difference suggests a possible causal link between peer-group cohesion and hierarchical social distance. 
> 
> The following hypothesis may thus be offered: an official who enjoys social integration among his peers will be more impersonal and detached in his relations with subordinates than one who does not. For peer-group support allows the superior to be more detached by obviating the need for courting the support of his subordinates. 
> 
> To test this hypothesis, we used data from both welfare agencies. Specifically, we examined the association between the index of detachment to subordinates already described and three measures of social integration among peers: whether the supervisor felt integrated among the other supervisors in his department; the number of "friendship choices" received from peers; and whether the supervisor spent off-duty time with other supervisors.

#### Excerpt 2: The Structural Dilemma: Coordination vs. Free Communication (Chapter 7 & 9, pp. 172–173, 242–244)

> **The Dilemma of Coordination and Free Communication.** A fundamental dilemma in formal organizations stems from the conflicting requirements of coordination and communication. Effective coordination of differentiated activities requires a hierarchical authority structure with centralized decision making. Such an authority structure, however, tends to restrict and distort the free flow of communication, particularly upward communication from subordinates to superiors.
> 
> Subordinates are naturally reluctant to communicate information that reflects unfavorably upon their own performance or that might displease their superiors. Consequently, the higher echelons of a tall hierarchy often receive an overly optimistic and filtered picture of operating reality at the bottom. While hierarchy solves the problem of coordination, it creates severe obstacles to reliable upward feedback, creating social and cognitive distance between organizational levels.

